# Go notes

Applies to `cmd/roast`, `internal/`, and Go tests. Notes for one area
are in that area's file (index in `AGENTS.md`).

## CI checks

- CI runs `go mod tidy` and fails on a diff: run it after removing code
  that was the last user of a dependency.
- CI runs staticcheck (`go tool -modfile=.github/tools/staticcheck/go.mod
  staticcheck ./...`); deleting a caller can leave an unused helper that
  fails U1000.
- `scripts/licenses.sh` special-cases notices for specific linked
  packages. When a dependency drops out of the `./cmd/roast` graph,
  check `go list -deps ./cmd/roast` for each target and update the
  script.
- CI runs `npm run test:go` with `GOFLAGS=-race`, so Go tests that spawn
  `go` subprocesses inherit `-race`; such a subprocess must not also set
  `CGO_ENABLED=0` for a build.

## CLI tests (`internal/cli`)

- Tests drive `ExecuteContext` against an `httptest` fake inference
  server; `executeContext(ctx, args, stdin, stdout, stderr)` lets a test
  answer prompts (`ExecuteContext` passes `os.Stdin`).
- `internal/testutil.ClearAmbientEnvironment` strips `ROAST_*`,
  `OPENAI_*`, `OPENROUTER_*` and `ANTHROPIC_*` in `TestMain`.
- A test that runs a review needs a `.roast.json`
  (`project_config_test.go`: `projectConfig`, `writeFile`, `chdir`,
  `newModelRecorder`). Profiles name a credential with `api_key_env` and
  set it with `t.Setenv`.
- The `httptest` server is a custom endpoint, so a review test approves
  first: `approveProjectConfig(t, ...)` (`trust_test.go`, runs
  `trust --yes`), or `settingsRepo`, which approves. A test asserting a
  config error approves a valid config and then edits it
  (`approvedThenEdited`) so a trust failure cannot mask the error.
- `roast doctor` skips endpoint probes for an unapproved config. A
  doctor test that probes a custom endpoint chdirs into a temp repo and
  approves there (`approveDoctorConfig`, `doctor_test.go`), never in
  this checkout.
- Untracked files are part of a review's diff, so a test that checks
  which file's text reached the fake server keeps decoys out of scope
  (for example via `.git/info/exclude`).
- `settings_test.go` has `requestRecorder` (records model,
  `Authorization`, body), `settingsRepo`, `reviewMeta` (reads the run's
  `meta.json`), and `redirectDefaultTransport`, which swaps
  `http.DefaultTransport` so a profile on a provider's default endpoint
  reaches the fake server.
- `init_test.go` has `providerServer`, a fake speaking the Responses
  (`/v1/responses`), Anthropic (`/v1/messages`), and OpenRouter chat
  (`/api/v1/chat/completions`) protocols; pair it with
  `redirectDefaultTransport(t, server.URL)`.
- Terminal detection goes through the package var
  `interactiveTerminals` (`prompt.go`); tests swap it with
  `simulateTerminals(t, ...)` (`trust_prompt_test.go`).
// tapzero's `test` plus Go-style `t.cleanup(fn)`: cleanups run in reverse
// order when the test body finishes, pass or fail.
import { test as tapTest, type Test } from '@substrate-system/tapzero'

export type CleanupTest = Test&{
    cleanup (fn:() => void|Promise<void>):void;
}

export function test (
    name:string,
    fn:(t:CleanupTest) => void|Promise<void>
):void {
    tapTest(name, async t => {
        const cleanups:(() => void|Promise<void>)[] = []
        const ct = Object.assign(t, {
            cleanup (f:() => void|Promise<void>):void { cleanups.push(f) },
        })
        try {
            await fn(ct)
        } finally {
            for (const f of cleanups.reverse()) await f()
        }
    })
}
